## Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd)

## The Problem

```
Time: T0 — popular product page cached for 1 hour
Time: T1 — cache expires
Time: T1+1ms — 500 concurrent users request the same page

→ All 500 miss the cache simultaneously
→ All 500 query the database: SELECT * FROM products WHERE id = '...' (expensive)
→ Database is hit 500× with the same query
→ Response time spikes, DB may crash
→ Eventually cache is rebuilt — but 500 queries already ran
```

## Solution 1: Cache Locking (Mutex)

```typescript
async function getProductWithLock(id: string): Promise<Product> {
  const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}`);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const lockKey = `lock:product:${id}`;
  const acquired = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', { NX: true, EX: 5 });

  if (acquired) {
    // This request wins the lock — fetch and cache
    const product = await db.getProduct(id);
    await redis.setEx(`product:${id}`, 3600, JSON.stringify(product));
    await redis.del(lockKey);
    return product;
  } else {
    // Others wait and retry — cache will be populated by the winner
    await sleep(50);
    return getProductWithLock(id); // retry
  }
}
```

## Solution 2: Probabilistic Early Recomputation

```typescript
// XFetch algorithm — recompute before expiry with increasing probability
function shouldRecompute(ttlRemaining: number, computeTime: number): boolean {
  const beta = 1.0; // tune: higher = more eager recomputation
  return Date.now() - computeTime * beta * Math.log(Math.random()) >= ttlRemaining;
}
```

## Solution 3: Stale-While-Revalidate

```typescript
async function getProduct(id: string) {
  const entry = await redis.get(`product:${id}`);
  if (!entry) return fetchAndCache(id); // cold cache

  const { data, expiresAt } = JSON.parse(entry);

  if (Date.now() > expiresAt) {
    // Stale — serve immediately, refresh in background
    fetchAndCache(id).catch(console.error); // fire and forget
  }

  return data; // always returns something — no stampede
}
```

## Solution 4: Background Refresh

```
A cron job or background process refreshes popular cache entries
before they expire — the cache is always warm.

Suitable for: predictable expiry, non-user-specific data (product catalogue)
Not suitable for: user-specific data (personalised caches)
```

## Fail Fast vs Robust Design

## Fail Fast

```
Philosophy: make bugs visible immediately at the source
Mechanism:  validate inputs, assert invariants, crash loudly

Benefits:
  ✅ Bugs are caught early, near the root cause
  ✅ No corrupt state propagating through the system
  ✅ Easier to debug — stack trace points to the problem

Risks:
  ❌ Crashes user requests for recoverable conditions
  ❌ Can be too aggressive in production
```

```typescript
// ✅ Fail fast: validate domain invariants immediately
class Money {
  constructor(public amount: number, public currency: string) {
    if (amount < 0) throw new Error('Amount cannot be negative');
    if (!['GBP', 'USD', 'EUR'].includes(currency)) throw new Error('Invalid currency');
  }
}

// ✅ Fail fast at startup: missing env var = don't start
if (!process.env.DATABASE_URL) {
  console.error('DATABASE_URL is required');
  process.exit(1);
}
```

## Robust Design

```
Philosophy: failures are expected; handle them without crashing
Mechanism:  try/catch, fallbacks, circuit breakers, retries

Benefits:
  ✅ Better user experience — graceful degradation
  ✅ Tolerates transient failures (network blips, timeouts)
  ✅ Suitable for distributed systems where partial failure is normal

Risks:
  ❌ Can hide bugs — swallowing errors silently
  ❌ System limps on in a degraded state — harder to detect
```

```typescript
// ✅ Robust: external call might fail transiently
async function getRecommendations(userId: string) {
  try {
    return await recommendationService.get(userId);
  } catch (err) {
    logger.warn('Recommendation service unavailable', { err });
    return getCachedRecommendations(userId) ?? getPopularProducts();
  }
}
```

## When to Apply Each

```
Fail Fast:
  - Domain invariants (business rules that must never be violated)
  - Application startup (missing config, schema mismatch)
  - Internal API contracts (wrong argument types)
  - Development/CI environments

Robust:
  - External API calls
  - Database reads (can serve stale cache)
  - User-facing request handlers
  - Background jobs (retry vs crash)
  - Production environments
```

## Repository Pattern vs Active Record

## Active Record (Smart Business Objects)

```typescript
// Domain object knows about persistence
class User extends ActiveRecord {
  static async findById(id: string): Promise<User | null> {
    return db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  }

  async save(): Promise<void> {
    await db.query('INSERT INTO users (id, email) VALUES ($1, $2)', [this.id, this.email]);
  }

  activate(): void {
    this.status = 'active';
  }
}

// Usage
const user = await User.findById(id);
user.activate();
await user.save();
```

```
✅ Simple, less boilerplate
✅ Great for CRUD-heavy apps (Rails, Eloquent)
❌ Domain tightly coupled to DB — can't test without DB
❌ Violates SRP: object knows both its business rules AND how to persist
❌ Hard to swap persistence layer
```

## Repository Pattern (Separated)

```typescript
// Domain object: pure, no DB knowledge
class User {
  constructor(public id: string, public email: string, public status: string) {}
  activate(): void { this.status = 'active'; }
}

// Repository: handles persistence
interface UserRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<User | null>;
  save(user: User): Promise<void>;
}

// Usage
const user = await userRepo.findById(id);
user.activate(); // pure domain logic — no DB call
await userRepo.save(user);
```

```
✅ Testable: inject a mock repo, test domain without DB
✅ Swappable persistence: switch from PG to MongoDB by implementing the interface
✅ Clear SRP: domain ≠ persistence
❌ More boilerplate
❌ Overkill for simple CRUD
```

## Decision Guide

```
Simple CRUD app, small team, fast delivery  → Active Record (Prisma, Drizzle)
Complex domain, DDD, testability matters   → Repository Pattern
Mixed: start with Active Record, extract repos when domain grows complex
```

## Grouping Repositories in the Repository Pattern

## The Rule: One Repository per Aggregate Root

```
Aggregate: cluster of domain objects treated as a transactional unit
Aggregate Root: the single entry point for accessing the aggregate

Repository: manages loading/saving the entire aggregate
```

## Example: E-Commerce Domain

```
Aggregates and their roots:

Order Aggregate Root: Order
  → contains: OrderItem (value object / entity)
  → has: OrderRepository
  ✅ NOT: OrderItemRepository (access items through Order)

Customer Aggregate Root: Customer
  → contains: Address (value object)
  → has: CustomerRepository

Product Aggregate Root: Product
  → contains: ProductVariant, ProductImage
  → has: ProductRepository
```

## Code Structure

```typescript
// ✅ Correct: Order aggregate root
interface OrderRepository {
  findById(id: OrderId): Promise<Order | null>;
  findByCustomer(customerId: CustomerId): Promise<Order[]>;
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
  // NOT: findOrderItem(itemId: string) — access via Order
}

// ✅ Correct: access child entities through the root
const order = await orderRepo.findById(orderId);
const item = order.findItem(itemId); // in-memory, not a DB query
order.removeItem(itemId);
await orderRepo.save(order); // saves Order + its items in one transaction

// ❌ Wrong: repository per table (leaks DB structure into domain)
interface OrderItemRepository {
  findByOrderId(orderId: string): Promise<OrderItem[]>; // bypasses the aggregate
}
```

## Anti-Pattern: Generic Repository

```typescript
// ❌ Don't expose raw query capabilities
interface GenericRepository<T> {
  findWhere(conditions: Record<string, unknown>): Promise<T[]>; // leaks SQL thinking
}

// ✅ Domain-specific methods only
interface OrderRepository {
  findPendingForCustomer(customerId: CustomerId): Promise<Order[]>;
  findPlacedBetween(from: Date, to: Date): Promise<Order[]>;
}
```

## Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

## Two Statements

```
1. High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.
   Both should depend on abstractions.

2. Abstractions should not depend on details.
   Details should depend on abstractions.
```

## Without DIP

```typescript
// High-level depends on low-level (concrete class)
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDatabase(); // ← tightly coupled

  async getOrder(id: string) {
    return this.db.query(`SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = '${id}'`);
  }
}

Problems:
  - Can't test OrderService without PostgreSQL
  - Can't switch to DynamoDB without rewriting OrderService
  - OrderService and PostgresDatabase must be deployed together
```

## With DIP

```typescript
// Abstraction defined in high-level module
interface OrderRepository { // defined in domain/application layer
  findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null>;
}

// High-level depends on abstraction
class OrderService {
  constructor(private repo: OrderRepository) {} // interface injected

  async getOrder(id: string): Promise<Order> {
    const order = await this.repo.findById(id);
    if (!order) throw new OrderNotFoundError(id);
    return order;
  }
}

// Low-level depends on abstraction (implements the interface)
class PostgresOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
  async findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null> {
    // PostgreSQL-specific code here
  }
}

// Wiring (composition root)
const service = new OrderService(new PostgresOrderRepository(db));

// Testing
const mockRepo: OrderRepository = { findById: async () => testOrder };
const testService = new OrderService(mockRepo); // no DB needed
```

## Why Important

```
✅ Testability: inject mocks, no real DB in unit tests
✅ Flexibility: swap PostgresOrderRepository for DynamoOrderRepository
✅ Stability: high-level business logic doesn't change when DB changes
✅ Parallel work: team A builds OrderService, team B builds the repo impl
✅ Clean Architecture / DDD: the inversion is the mechanism
        that keeps domain code free from infrastructure
```

## Repository and Unit of Work

## Repository: Single Aggregate Scope

```typescript
// Each repository manages one aggregate
interface OrderRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null>;
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
}

interface InventoryRepository {
  findByProduct(productId: string): Promise<Inventory | null>;
  save(inventory: Inventory): Promise<void>;
}
```

## The Problem: Cross-Aggregate Transactions

```typescript
// ❌ Without UoW: hard to coordinate atomicity
async function placeOrder(command: PlaceOrderCommand): Promise<void> {
  const order = Order.create(command);
  await orderRepo.save(order); // what if inventory save fails?

  const inventory = await inventoryRepo.findByProduct(command.productId);
  inventory.deduct(command.quantity);
  await inventoryRepo.save(inventory); // if this fails, order was already saved!
}
```

## With Unit of Work

```typescript
interface UnitOfWork {
  orders: OrderRepository;
  inventory: InventoryRepository;
  payments: PaymentRepository;
  commit(): Promise<void>;
  rollback(): Promise<void>;
}

async function placeOrder(command: PlaceOrderCommand, uow: UnitOfWork): Promise<void> {
  const order = Order.create(command);
  uow.orders.save(order); // registers change — does NOT hit DB yet

  const inventory = await uow.inventory.findByProduct(command.productId);
  inventory.deduct(command.quantity);
  uow.inventory.save(inventory); // registers change

  await uow.commit(); // ALL changes written in ONE transaction
  // If commit fails: NOTHING was written — atomic
}
```

## PostgreSQL Implementation

```typescript
class PostgresUnitOfWork implements UnitOfWork {
  private client: PoolClient;

  constructor(private pool: Pool) {}

  async begin(): Promise<void> {
    this.client = await this.pool.connect();
    await this.client.query('BEGIN');
    this.orders = new PostgresOrderRepository(this.client);
    this.inventory = new PostgresInventoryRepository(this.client);
  }

  async commit(): Promise<void> {
    await this.client.query('COMMIT');
    this.client.release();
  }

  async rollback(): Promise<void> {
    await this.client.query('ROLLBACK');
    this.client.release();
  }
}
```

## Summary

```
Repository → one aggregate, persistence abstraction
Unit of Work → coordinates multiple repositories in one transaction
```

## Failure vs Error

## Error: Expected, Handled

```typescript
// Errors are part of the domain/application contract
// They're anticipated and part of the API's return shape

// Domain error: business rule violation
if (order.status !== 'pending') {
  return { error: 'INVALID_STATUS', message: 'Order cannot be cancelled' };
}

// Not-found error: normal outcome of a lookup
const user = await userRepo.findById(id);
if (!user) return { error: 'NOT_FOUND', message: 'User not found' };

// Validation error: expected user input problem
if (!isValidEmail(email)) {
  return { error: 'VALIDATION', field: 'email', message: 'Invalid email format' };
}

// All of these: normal flow, handled gracefully, returned to caller
```

## Failure: Unexpected, Escalated

```typescript
// Failures are infrastructure/environment problems
// They're NOT part of the normal business flow

try {
  const result = await db.query('SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  return result;
} catch (err) {
  // Database connection failed — this is a FAILURE
  // Not a domain concept, not expected in normal flow
  logger.error('Database query failed', { err, query: 'getOrder', id });
  metrics.increment('db.failures');
  throw new InfrastructureError('Database unavailable'); // escalate
}
```

## Summary

```
              Error                     Failure
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Nature        Expected, anticipated      Unexpected, exceptional
Example       User not found, invalid    DB down, disk full, OOM
              input, permission denied   network unreachable
Handling      Return/throw within        Retry, circuit break,
              normal flow               escalate, alert operator
API contract  Yes — part of the contract No — out of the ordinary
In code       Result type, typed errors  Try/catch, catch-all, alert
```

## Isolating Domain Entities from Presentation Layer

## What Happens Without Isolation

```typescript
// ❌ Domain entity exposed directly to the API
app.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await userRepo.findById(req.params.id);
  res.json(user); // exposes ALL fields of the User entity
});

// User entity might contain:
// { id, email, passwordHash, internalAccountId,
//   auditLog, stripeCustomerId, isDeleted, ... }
// → passwordHash and internal fields leak to the client!
```

```typescript
// ❌ Presentation concerns leak into the domain
class User {
  email: string;
  displayName: string;     // UI formatting concern in domain
  formattedJoinDate: string; // display formatting in domain
  avatarUrl?: string;      // optional for form — now in domain
}
// UI change → domain entity change → violates SRP
```

## With Isolation (Entity → DTO → ViewModel)

```typescript
// Domain entity: pure business rules
class User {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    private email: string,
    private passwordHash: string, // private!
    public readonly createdAt: Date,
  ) {}

  isAdmin(): boolean { /* business rule */ }
}

// DTO: safe for API transport
interface UserDTO {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  createdAt: string; // serialised for JSON
  // NO passwordHash — mapping layer controls what's exposed
}

// ViewModel: shaped for specific UI view
interface UserProfileViewModel {
  displayName: string;
  joinedText: string;  // 'Joined 3 months ago'
  avatarUrl: string | null;
}

// Mapping layer
function toUserDTO(user: User): UserDTO {
  return { id: user.id, email: user.email, createdAt: user.createdAt.toISOString() };
}
```

## Benefits

```
✅ Security: only explicitly mapped fields are exposed
✅ SRP: domain changes ≠ UI changes
✅ Independent evolution: domain and UI can change for their own reasons
✅ API stability: internal model can refactor without breaking API consumers
```
